# ROOTS19 Architecture Update — Governed Composition Across Runtime Environments

**Author:** Miles Millstone  
**Project:** ROOTS19 / Governed Cognition Substrate  
**Related implementation:** Millstar Creative Engine (MCE)  
**Status:** Exploratory architecture — implemented and under continued development

---

## Overview

After approximately two years of iterative development, implementation, and testing, a recurring architectural principle within ROOTS19 has now been independently demonstrated in a substantially different environment: the web.

ROOTS19 and the Millstar Creative Engine (MCE) are different systems with different purposes.

They are not being merged.

What they demonstrate is a shared architectural principle:

> **Preserve the governed functional object while allowing the environment, composition, and presentation around that object to change.**

ROOTS19 originally explored this principle through a portable governed substrate capable of operating across different host environments.

MCE now demonstrates a related form of portability inside a browser-based creative runtime.

---

## ROOTS19

ROOTS19 is a governed cognition substrate.

Its architecture separates stable substrate responsibilities from higher runtime cognition and operational behavior.

A simplified view is:

    Stable Governed Substrate
              ↓
       Runtime Cognition
              ↓
    Operational Cognition
              ↓
        Host Environment

The host environment may change without requiring the governed architecture itself to be redefined.

This allows the system to preserve identity, authority, continuity, and behavioral boundaries while operating within different environments.

Nova is an example of a personality/profile operating on the ROOTS19 substrate rather than being the substrate itself.

---

## MCE

The Millstar Creative Engine approaches the same architectural problem from a different direction.

Instead of cognition across operating environments, MCE deals with creative objects, capabilities, scenes, presentations, and experiences across changing browser/runtime conditions.

A simplified MCE composition can be represented as:

    Governed Object
          ↓
       Controller
          ↓
         Scene
          ↓
      Presentation
          ↓
       Experience

The implementation can then be composed differently according to its runtime environment:

    Desktop
    Landscape
    Portrait
    Mobile
    Alternate Schema
    Alternate Experience

The underlying functional pieces do not need to be rewritten simply because their composition changes.

