## Packet-Based Composition

During development, the same organizational pattern has repeatedly appeared at different scales.

    OBJECT
        → governed members

    SCENE
        → governed objects, motion, lighting, effects

    CAPABILITY
        → governed functional components

    SECTION
        → governed capabilities and presentation

    PAGE / EXPERIENCE
        → governed sections

    RUNTIME
        → governed compositions

These can be treated conceptually as packets.

A packet establishes a stable boundary around a functional unit.

The implementation inside that boundary can remain stable while a higher composition layer determines:

- where it participates;
- what it is associated with;
- how it is presented;
- what other packets surround it;
- which runtime uses it;
- and whether another compatible implementation occupies its position.

The higher layer therefore composes the system rather than rewriting its members.

---

## Recursive Composition

An important result of the work is that this model does not appear limited to one scale.

The same basic architecture can recur:

    member
       ↓
    object packet
       ↓
    scene packet
       ↓
    capability packet
       ↓
    section packet
       ↓
    experience packet
       ↓
    runtime composition

This creates a recursive composition model.

Each level can become a stable component of the level above it.

The result is not simply modular code.

The objective is **governed modularity**:

> A component retains a defined identity, responsibility, interface, and authority boundary while participating in larger compositions.

