## Two Implementations of the Principle

ROOTS19 and MCE now provide two substantially different exploratory implementations.

### ROOTS19

    governed substrate
            ↓
    different host/runtime conditions
            ↓
    preserved cognitive architecture

### MCE

    governed functional components
            ↓
    different composition/presentation conditions
            ↓
    preserved implementation

This distinction is important.

ROOTS19 is not a web presentation engine.

MCE is not a cognition substrate.

The value of comparing them is that the architectural principle has survived implementation in two very different problem domains.

---

## Why This Matters

Traditional software frequently binds implementation, environment, presentation, and composition tightly enough that changing one eventually requires modification of the others.

The architecture being explored here attempts to reduce that coupling.

Instead of:

    environment change
            ↓
    implementation rewrite

the goal is:

    stable governed implementation
            ↓
    explicit composition boundary
            ↓
    environment-specific assembly

This can allow increasingly large systems to be constructed from smaller stable systems without requiring every higher-level change to propagate downward through the implementation.

---

## Current Status

This work should be considered an **exploratory system architecture**.

It has developed through approximately two years of practical construction, testing, failure analysis, redesign, and implementation across ROOTS19 and related Millstar systems.

The purpose of publishing the architecture is not to claim ownership of modular programming, component systems, scene graphs, or other established software concepts.

The purpose is to document the specific governed composition architecture developed through this work, establish its terminology and implementation history, and make the system available for further technical exploration.

---

## Core Principle

The current architecture can be summarized in one statement:

> **Preserve the governed functional object. Vary the environment and composition around it.**

ROOTS19 demonstrates this principle through governed cognition and runtime portability.

MCE demonstrates it through governed objects, capabilities, sections, scenes, schemas, and browser-based experience composition.

Together they provide two independent implementation paths for continued investigation of governed, portable, recursively composable systems.

